<h2>The Brief Grew with the Business</h2>

<p>Reve Stitching needed a credible digital presence, but the work quickly became more than a marketing site. Quote requests, sample requests, product information, internal follow-up, and client communication all touched the same customer journey.</p>

<p>The important shift was treating the website as operational software: every public form needed a clear destination, every status needed ownership, and every useful interface needed to keep working when an optional integration failed.</p>

<h2>Picking the Stack</h2>

<p>After researching for about a week, I landed on a stack that made sense for a content-heavy B2B website:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Astro</strong> for fast content delivery and selective interactivity.</li>
  <li><strong>Tailwind CSS</strong> for styling. Utility classes let me move fast without fighting CSS specificity.</li>
  <li><strong>GSAP + ScrollTrigger</strong> for deliberate, performance-aware motion.</li>
  <li><strong>Supabase and libSQL/Drizzle</strong> for data-backed workflows.</li>
  <li><strong>Vercel</strong> for deployment and server-side routes.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What I Actually Built</h2>

<p>The website ended up being much bigger than a simple corporate page. Here's what shipped:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Quote, sample, and contact intake flows</li>
  <li>Admin operations for requests, chat, email templates, and follow-ups</li>
  <li>A client portal for project-facing updates</li>
  <li>ERPNext integration points for operational continuity</li>
  <li>Turnstile, validation, transactional email, and error monitoring</li>
  <li>Responsive public experiences with motion that respects reduced-motion preferences</li>
</ul>

<p>The site is live at <a href="https://revestitching.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">revestitching.com</a> if you want to see it.</p>

<h2>The Hard Parts</h2>

<p>Motion was one of the visible challenges. Scroll-triggered reveals had to feel intentional without making slower devices pay an unnecessary cost. The durable lesson was that fewer, better-timed movements create more confidence than constant animation.</p>

<p>The harder work was behind the interface: validating input, keeping privileged tools separate, connecting notifications, and designing failure states so an unavailable service did not take down the rest of the journey.</p>

<h2>Architecture Is Also Product Work</h2>

<p>The current codebase contains 38 API route files, 13 admin pages, and 13 portal pages. Those numbers matter only because they expose the real shape of the product: the system has multiple audiences, permissions, and operational handoffs.</p>

<p>Counting pages is not a substitute for outcomes. It is a reminder to design navigation, data ownership, and error handling before small implementation decisions harden into constraints.</p>

<h2>What I'd Do Differently</h2>

<p>If I started again, I would map public intake, internal ownership, and client-visible status before writing interface code. That model is more useful than a sitemap alone because it catches operational gaps early.</p>

<p>But honestly? Shipping something imperfect taught me more than any tutorial ever could.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>A production platform succeeds when the interface and the operation behind it tell the same story.</p>
</blockquote>

